      <header class="cod-title-block">
        <p class="cod-chapter-label">Chapter Eight · Christian or Demonic?</p>
        <h1 class="cod-title">The Tithe</h1>
        <p class="cod-subtitle">Into Whose Storehouse?</p>
        <p class="cod-tagline">The church calls itself the storehouse and the pastor the priest. Scripture says the temple and the priest are you.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="cod-pullquote">Christian or demonic? Let Scripture and history decide.</p>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Introduction</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Question No One Is Asking</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">The plates are about to be passed. Before they go out, someone reads Malachi 3: "Will a man rob God? Yet ye have robbed me … In tithes and offerings. Ye are cursed with a curse." Then the promise: "Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse … and prove me now herewith … if I will not open you the windows of heaven." Then the band starts to play, soft and slow, as the ushers move down the rows. The envelopes go in. Ten percent of the paycheck, before taxes. At the end of the year a giving statement comes in the mail, ready for the tax return.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Behind that moment sit three beliefs almost no one questions. <strong>The church is the storehouse</strong> — the storehouse for its members and for the whole community. <strong>The pastor is the priest</strong> — the Levite of the congregation, who lives from the tithe. <strong>And the building is the house of God</strong>, which the tithe and the building fund must keep up.</p>
      <p class="cod-body"><strong>This is not a study against the tithe.</strong> The tithe is in the Law of Yahuah (Leviticus 27:30), and Yahushua upheld it: "these ought ye to have done" (Matthew 23:23). But the tithe was built around three things — a temple, a priesthood, and the outsiders it fed. Get those wrong and the tithe goes to the wrong place. So this chapter asks three questions all the way through. <strong>Who is the temple? Who is the priest? Who are the outsiders?</strong> Until those are settled in Scripture, the tithe cannot be understood at all.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">And there is something strange about it. The same churches that teach the Law was nailed to the cross — no Sabbath, no feasts, no clean and unclean — have kept one commandment out of it, and they keep it every week. The Sabbath is gone. The feasts are gone. The tithe stays. They read Malachi 3 over the plate, but stop before Malachi 4, where Yahuah says only a few verses later, "Remember ye the law of Moses my servant, which I commanded unto him in Horeb for all Israel, with the statutes and judgments" (Malachi 4:4). <strong>Out of the whole Law, the one command the church kept is the one that pays the church.</strong></p>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part One</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Tithe Yahuah Gave</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">Start with the Law, because everything after it is measured against it.</p>
      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>What it was.</strong> The increase of the land and the flock: "the seed of the land, or … the fruit of the tree," and "the tithe of the herd, or of the flock" (Leviticus 27:30, 32). Grain, wine, oil, and animals. A family far from the place of worship could turn it into money for the trip, but there it was spent again on food (Deuteronomy 14:24-26).</li>
        <li><strong>The place.</strong> The tithe was brought to "the place which Yahuah shall choose" (Deuteronomy 14:23), and the storehouse was the set of temple chambers where the grain, wine, and oil were kept (Nehemiah 13:5, 12) — "that there may be meat in mine house" (Malachi 3:10). Food, not a building fund.</li>
        <li><strong>The priesthood.</strong> The tribe of Levi had no land. "I have given the children of Levi all the tenth in Israel for an inheritance, for their service" (Numbers 18:21). The Levites gave a tenth of that to the priests (Numbers 18:26).</li>
        <li><strong>The giver himself.</strong> The tithe was eaten at the feast: "thou shalt rejoice, thou, and thine household, and the Levite that is within thy gates" (Deuteronomy 14:26-27).</li>
        <li><strong>The outsiders.</strong> Every third year the tithe was laid up in the towns, and "the stranger, and the fatherless, and the widow … shall come, and shall eat and be satisfied" (Deuteronomy 14:28-29).</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="cod-subsection">"But We Don't Farm Anymore"</h3>
      <p class="cod-body">The usual answer at this point is that Israel was a farming nation, so the tithe was paid in food; today we work for wages, so the tithe is paid in money. It sounds reasonable. But Israel was never only farmers, and it was never without money. Abraham paid "four hundred shekels of silver, current money with the merchant" for a field (Genesis 23:16). Israel had craftsmen, smiths, weavers, and builders (Exodus 31:1-6). In Yahushua's day men worked for a day's wage in coin (Matthew 20:2); Peter was a fisherman, Yahushua was a carpenter (Mark 6:3), Paul made tents (Acts 18:3), and Matthew collected taxes. Not one of them is ever told to tithe his wages.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">The Law even planned for money. If the journey was too far to carry the tithe, "then shalt thou turn it into money" — and when you arrived, "thou shalt bestow that money for whatsoever thy soul lusteth after, for oxen, or for sheep, or for wine," and eat it there before Yahuah with your household and the Levite (Deuteronomy 14:25-27). <strong>When the tithe became money, the Law turned it back into a meal.</strong> The form could change. The purpose could not: the feast, the Levite, and the outsiders.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Giving from wages is not wrong. Paul's collection was money, laid by "as God hath prospered him" (1 Corinthians 16:2) — and it went to the poor saints (Romans 15:26). But notice what the objection does. If the tithe can be updated because times have changed, so could the Sabbath and the feasts — and the church says those are gone. <strong>It updates the Law where updating pays, and cancels it where keeping it would cost.</strong></p>
      <p class="cod-body">Malachi fits inside that picture. The book is aimed at the priests (Malachi 1:6; 2:1). And five verses before "Will a man rob God?" Yahuah says whom He will judge: those who "oppress the hireling in his wages, the widow, and the fatherless, and that turn aside the stranger" (Malachi 3:5).</p>
      <p class="cod-pullquote">A temple, a priesthood, a feast, and the outsiders at the gate. That was the tithe. Now look at each one through the eyes of the New Testament.</p>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Two</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Through New Testament Eyes</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Who Is the Temple?</h3>
      <p class="cod-body">Paul asked it plainly: "Know ye not that ye are the temple of God, and that the Spirit of God dwelleth in you?" (1 Corinthians 3:16). "Your body is the temple" (1 Corinthians 6:19). Believers are "builded together for an habitation of God through the Spirit" (Ephesians 2:22), "lively stones … built up a spiritual house" (1 Peter 2:5). Stephen said it to the men who owned the most famous building on earth: "the most High dwelleth not in temples made with hands" (Acts 7:48). <strong>The house of Yahuah is not on the corner of Main Street. It is His people.</strong></p>
      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Who Is the Priest?</h3>
      <p class="cod-body">Peter wrote to ordinary believers: "ye are … a royal priesthood" (1 Peter 2:9). Yahushua "hath made us kings and priests unto God" (Revelation 1:6). Over all of them stands one High Priest, Yahushua, "after the order of Melchisedec" (Hebrews 6:20) — and Hebrews ties the tithe to that order. Abraham gave a tenth to Melchizedek, and "here men that die receive tithes; but there he receiveth them, of whom it is witnessed that he liveth" (Hebrews 7:8). In the priesthood of Melchizedek, the One who receives the tithe is the One who lives forever. <strong>No pastor is a Levite, and no pastor is Melchizedek.</strong></p>
      <h3 class="cod-subsection">"They Which Preach the Gospel Should Live of the Gospel"</h3>
      <p class="cod-body">This is the verse every pastor stands on, and many believers do too: if a man preaches the Word, the people should support him. It deserves a full answer, because Paul really did say it. "Even so hath the Master ordained that they which preach the gospel should live of the gospel" (1 Corinthians 9:14). He also wrote, "Let the elders that rule well be counted worthy of double honour, especially they who labour in the word and doctrine" (1 Timothy 5:17). Read both where they stand, and five things come out of the text.</p>
      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>It is a right, not a tithe.</strong> Paul argues from a soldier, a vineyard, a flock, and an ox that is not muzzled (1 Corinthians 9:7-9). He never mentions a tenth, never sets a percentage, never ties it to a building, and never threatens a curse.</li>
        <li><strong>What it covered was food and lodging for men on the road.</strong> Paul is defending the right of traveling apostles — "the other apostles, and … the brethren of the Master, and Cephas" — to "eat and to drink" and to bring a wife on the journey (1 Corinthians 9:4-5). It is the same right Yahushua gave the men He sent out two by two: "in the same house remain, eating and drinking such things as they give: for the labourer is worthy of his hire" (Luke 10:7). He also told them, "Provide neither gold, nor silver, nor brass in your purses … for the workman is worthy of his meat" (Matthew 10:9-10). His meat — his food. Not a salary, a house, and a pension.</li>
        <li><strong>Even the altar comparison is food.</strong> "They which wait at the altar are partakers with the altar" (1 Corinthians 9:13). The priests ate their portion of the sacrifices. "Even so" means the same kind of thing: a share of the table, not a tenth of everyone's wages.</li>
        <li><strong>Paul refused it, and told the church to copy him.</strong> "We have not used this power" (1 Corinthians 9:12). "I have used none of these things … it were better for me to die, than that any man should make my glorying void" (9:15). His reward was "that, when I preach the gospel, I may make the gospel of Messiah without charge" (9:18). To the Thessalonians he wrote, "Neither did we eat any man's bread for nought; but wrought with labour and travail night and day … Not because we have not power, but to make ourselves an ensample unto you to follow us" (2 Thessalonians 3:8-9). <strong>The right is in the text. So is the example — and the example is what he told them to follow.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>He said it straight to the pastors.</strong> When Paul said goodbye to the elders of Ephesus — the men he had told to "feed the church of God" (Acts 20:28) — his last instruction was not about their pay. "I have coveted no man's silver, or gold … these hands have ministered unto my necessities … I have shewed you all things, how that so labouring ye ought to support the weak, and to remember the words of the Master Yahushua, how he said, It is more blessed to give than to receive" (Acts 20:33-35). <strong>The shepherds were to work with their hands and support the weak — not be supported by them.</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="cod-body">Scripture also warns about what happens when the order is reversed. "Woe be to the shepherds of Israel that do feed themselves! should not the shepherds feed the flocks? Ye eat the fat, and ye clothe you with the wool … but ye feed not the flock" (Ezekiel 34:2-3). Micah said of Jerusalem's leaders, "the priests thereof teach for hire, and the prophets thereof divine for money" (Micah 3:11). Peter told the elders to feed the flock "not for filthy lucre, but of a ready mind" (1 Peter 5:2). And to anyone who stops working and calls it ministry, Paul left a plain rule: "if any would not work, neither should he eat … that with quietness they work, and eat their own bread" (2 Thessalonians 3:10, 12).</p>
      <p class="cod-body">So the honest answer is this. A believer may gladly share his table with a man who teaches him the Word (Galatians 6:6), and the assembly may help an elder who labors hard in it (1 Timothy 5:17). That is a gift from a willing heart. But nothing in the text makes the teacher a Levite, makes the tithe his salary, or lets him leave his work and live off the flock. The pattern Paul left for the shepherds was the opposite.</p>
      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Who Are the Outsiders?</h3>
      <p class="cod-body">The stranger, the fatherless, and the widow did not disappear at the cross. Yahushua said, "I was a stranger, and ye took me in … Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me" (Matthew 25:35, 40). James called it pure religion "to visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction" (James 1:27). The apostles asked Paul for one thing: "that we should remember the poor" (Galatians 2:10).</p>
      <p class="cod-pullquote">The temple is the believer. The priest is the believer, under one High Priest who lives. The outsiders are still at the gate. Every part of the history that follows moved the tithe away from all three.</p>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Three</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Priests Again (A.D. 100-400)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">The shift began early. A church manual from around A.D. 100, called the <em>Didache</em>, told believers to give the firstfruits of their wine, grain, and flocks to their prophets — "for they are your high priests." About A.D. 255, Cyprian, bishop of Carthage, pointed to the Levites, who "received tithes from the eleven tribes for their sustenance," and wrote, "The same principle holds now for the clergy." By then the bishop was being called <em>sacerdos</em>, priest. The office Scripture gave to every believer was being handed back to a few men.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Then the empire stepped in. In 313 Constantine ended the persecution of the church. He freed the clergy from public duties, gave them a place in the state, and in 321 made it lawful for churches to receive inheritances. He paid for grand church buildings — the first great basilicas in Rome and Jerusalem. Now the church had priests, it had buildings, and it had an income.</p>
      <div class="cod-warning-block">
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Christian?</strong> The believer is the priest (1 Peter 2:9) and the temple (1 Corinthians 3:16). There is one High Priest, and He lives (Hebrews 7:8, 24-25).</p>
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Or demonic?</strong> Scripture records a king who did exactly this. When Israel split, Jeroboam feared the people would go up to Jerusalem to worship and bring their offerings there. So he built his own houses of worship at Bethel and Dan, "and made priests of the lowest of the people, which were not of the sons of Levi," and set up a feast of his own choosing (1 Kings 12:27-33). Scripture says, "this thing became a sin" (1 Kings 12:30). <strong>A house built by men, a priesthood chosen by men, and the people's offerings kept from going where Yahuah sent them — that is Jeroboam's pattern.</strong> The church did not need to deny that the believer is a priest. It only needed to hire one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Four</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Tithe Becomes Law (A.D. 494-1977)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">Once the church had priests and buildings, the tithe followed. Around 494, Pope Gelasius divided church income into four parts: one for the bishop, one for the clergy, one for the buildings, and one for the poor. The poor were now one share out of four.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Then the tithe became law. In 567 the Council of Tours urged believers to pay it. In 585 the Council of Mâcon ordered it, and those who refused were to be excommunicated. Around 779 Charlemagne made it the law of his empire. Across Europe, great tithe barns were built to hold the grain the church collected — a storehouse in the most literal sense, now belonging to the church instead of to the widow.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">The Reformation broke with Rome but kept the tithe. The Protestant Church of England collected tithes from every landholder in the parish, whatever church he attended. Quakers and Baptists who refused were fined, had their goods seized, and were jailed; in 1753 the Quakers published two thick volumes recording their "sufferings." In Ireland, farmers forced to pay tithes to a church they did not attend rose up in the Tithe War of 1831-1836. England replaced the tithe with a land charge in 1836 and did not end the last payments until 1977.</p>
      <div class="cod-warning-block">
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Christian?</strong> Yahuah asked for offerings from the willing heart: "of every man that giveth it willingly with his heart ye shall take my offering" (Exodus 25:2). Paul said, "not grudgingly, or of necessity" (2 Corinthians 9:7).</p>
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Or demonic?</strong> Scripture records who takes a tenth by force. When Israel demanded a king, Yahuah told Samuel, "they have rejected me, that I should not reign over them" (1 Samuel 8:7). Then Samuel warned what that king would do: "He will take the tenth of your seed … He will take the tenth of your sheep: and ye shall be his servants" (1 Samuel 8:15, 17). <strong>A tenth taken by force is the mark of the king men set up in Yahuah's place.</strong> When the church took the tithe by threat of excommunication, fines, and jail, it was no longer collecting Yahuah's portion. It was collecting the king's.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Five</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Storehouse Comes to America (1600s-1920s)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">In colonial America, most colonies taxed their people to support an official church, and some states kept doing it after independence. Massachusetts was the last to stop, in 1833. After that, American churches had to pay their own way. Through much of the 1800s many did it by renting pews, with the best seats going to the families who paid the most.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Then a movement came to bring the tithe back. In 1876 a Chicago businessman named Thomas Kane began printing tithing pamphlets and mailing them free to pastors, signing himself simply "Layman." By 1903 he wrote that pastors had ordered his tithing literature "in quantities aggregating many millions." The youth movement joined in: Christian Endeavor's "Tenth Legion" pledge was signed by more than 25,000 young people.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">With the tithe came a new teaching about where it belonged, often called <strong>storehouse tithing.</strong> The local church is the storehouse of Malachi 3. The full tenth of each member's income belongs to it first. Gifts to a neighbor in need, a missionary, or a widow down the street come only after the tithe has been paid to the church — which then sees itself as the storehouse for the whole community. That is how most American churches teach the tithe today.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">The church also made up its own meanings for the words. Ask where the poor fit in, and many will say: the tithe is for the church, and the "offerings" above the tithe are the freewill gifts that go to the widows and the needy. Scripture has it backwards from that. <strong>The tithe itself was the part that fed the widow</strong> — "the stranger, and the fatherless, and the widow … shall come, and shall eat and be satisfied" (Deuteronomy 14:29; 26:12). Freewill offerings were gifts brought to Yahuah over and above what He commanded (Deuteronomy 16:10). The church kept the tithe for itself and left the widow the leftovers.</p>
      <div class="cod-warning-block">
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Christian?</strong> Malachi's storehouse held food so that the Levite, the stranger, the fatherless, and the widow could "eat and be satisfied" (Deuteronomy 14:29). In the new covenant the temple is the believer, the priest is the believer, and the outsiders are the poor at his own gate (Matthew 25:35-40). The storehouse goes where the temple goes.</p>
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Or demonic?</strong> Watch what Yahushua did in Mark 12. He warned against the scribes "which devour widows' houses" (Mark 12:40). Then He watched a poor widow drop into the temple treasury "all that she had, even all her living" (Mark 12:44). The very next thing He did was walk out and say that not one stone of that building would be left standing (Mark 13:1-2). <strong>The Law built a tithe to feed the widow. The system He walked out of taught the widow to feed it.</strong> Yahushua named the power behind that: "Ye cannot serve God and mammon" — and the Pharisees, "who were covetous," heard Him and "derided him" (Luke 16:13-14). Mammon is not a coin. It is a master.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Six</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Caesar's Receipt (1913-1993)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">In 1913 the United States began taxing income. Churches had been exempt from such taxes from the start, but in 1917, to keep giving up during the First World War, Congress let taxpayers deduct their gifts to churches and charities. From that year on, the tithe could lower a man's taxes. In 1921 Congress also stopped taxing the value of a parsonage given to a minister.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">In 1954 Congress rewrote the whole tax code. Churches were placed in a section called <strong>501(c)(3)</strong>, along with charities and schools. In the same law, Senator Lyndon Johnson added a rule, now called the Johnson Amendment: an organization in 501(c)(3) may not support or oppose any candidate for office. The pulpit now had a rule set by Washington. The same 1954 code also let ministers receive a cash housing allowance free of income tax.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Churches are treated as 501(c)(3) automatically; they do not have to apply. But most incorporate under state law as nonprofit corporations, many apply for an official letter from the Internal Revenue Service, and all of them issue receipts. Since 1993, any single gift of $250 or more needs a written receipt from the church before the giver can deduct it. That is why the giving statement arrives every January.</p>
      <div class="cod-warning-block">
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Christian?</strong> Yahushua taught that giving belongs in secret: "Take heed that ye do not your alms before men … let not thy left hand know what thy right hand doeth: That thine alms may be in secret: and thy Father which seeth in secret himself shall reward thee openly" (Matthew 6:1, 3-4).</p>
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Or demonic?</strong> Now the gift is recorded, receipted, and reported to the government so the giver can be paid back on his taxes. Of those who give to be seen, Yahushua said, "Verily I say unto you, They have their reward" (Matthew 6:2). And Scripture shows what happens when Yahuah's house reshapes itself to please the empire. King Ahaz went to Damascus to meet the king of Assyria, copied the altar he saw there, rebuilt Yahuah's house to match it, and changed its arrangements "for the king of Assyria" (2 Kings 16:10-18). <strong>The church gained a tax break. In exchange, it took a second body chartered by Caesar, and accepted Caesar's rule over what may be said from its pulpit.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Seven</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Building Fund</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">Then there is the building. Most churches carry a building fund, and many run capital campaigns that ask members for pledges over three to five years, on top of the tithe. Some churches have kept a building fund open for decades without ever buying a building. Others now meet in buildings worth millions.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">So who owns the building the members paid for? Rarely the pastor in his own name. It is usually the church corporation — governed by a board that, in some churches, the pastor and his own family control. In many denominations, it is not even the local church. The United Methodist Church has held every congregation's property "in trust" for the denomination since the days of John Wesley; members could leave, but the building stayed. When a door opened from 2019 to 2023, more than 7,600 Methodist congregations, about a quarter of the denomination in the United States, had to meet financial conditions and win a two-thirds vote to leave with the buildings they had paid for. In the Seventh-day Adventist Church, the title to local church property is held by the regional conference. The members give. Someone else holds the deed.</p>
      <div class="cod-warning-block">
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Christian?</strong> "Howbeit the most High dwelleth not in temples made with hands" (Acts 7:48). Paul told the men of Athens the same: Yahuah "dwelleth not in temples made with hands; neither is worshipped with men's hands, as though he needed any thing" (Acts 17:24-25). His house is His people (Ephesians 2:22).</p>
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Or demonic?</strong> The first great building project in Scripture was Babel: "let us build us a city and a tower, whose top may reach unto heaven; and let us make us a name" (Genesis 11:4). It gathered the people into one place, under one name, around one building. <strong>A building fund that never builds, and a building the givers do not own, serve the same purpose Babel did: they keep the people's eyes on a structure and their offerings flowing toward it — and away from the temple Yahuah actually lives in.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Eight</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Seed Faith (1950s-Today)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">In the second half of the twentieth century, the tithe picked up a promise it never had in the Law. In the 1950s the healing evangelist Oral Roberts needed money for his tent meetings, his radio broadcasts, and then his television program. He taught supporters to give to his ministry as a "seed" and to expect God to multiply it back. By 1970 he had a book called <em>Miracle of Seed-Faith</em>. In 1987 he told supporters that God would "call him home" unless they sent eight million dollars within a few months. The idea spread across television ministries and into ordinary churches: "sow a seed," and Malachi 3:10 becomes a money-back guarantee.</p>
      <div class="cod-warning-block">
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Christian?</strong> "Freely ye have received, freely give" (Matthew 10:8). When Simon the sorcerer offered money for the gift of the Spirit, Peter said, "Thy money perish with thee, because thou hast thought that the gift of God may be purchased with money" (Acts 8:20).</p>
        <p class="cod-body"><strong>Or demonic?</strong> Elisha took nothing from Naaman for his healing, but Gehazi ran after him and took the silver — and the leprosy clung to him (2 Kings 5:16, 20-27). Peter warned of teachers who "through covetousness … with feigned words make merchandise of you," following "the way of Balaam … who loved the wages of unrighteousness" (2 Peter 2:3, 15). And the bargain itself is the tempter's: "All these things will I give thee, if thou wilt fall down and worship me" (Matthew 4:9). <strong>Give me something, and I will give you more. That is not the Father's voice.</strong> And when the threat of a curse is used to pry the money loose, the voice is the accuser's (Revelation 12:10).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Nine</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Scripture Shows Instead</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">The tithe was never cancelled (Matthew 23:23). Read through New Testament eyes, it looks like this:</p>
      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>The temple is your house and your body.</strong> Yahuah's tenth is set apart there, by the priest who lives there — you (1 Corinthians 3:16; 1 Peter 2:9).</li>
        <li><strong>The feast is still kept.</strong> The tithe that was eaten "before Yahuah" with joy is still for keeping His appointed times with your household, and for bringing others to the table: "the Levite, the stranger, and the fatherless, and the widow" (Deuteronomy 16:11, 14).</li>
        <li><strong>The outsiders are fed.</strong> The widow, the orphan, and the stranger at your own gate are the storehouse's first purpose (Deuteronomy 14:28-29; James 1:27; Matthew 25:35-40).</li>
        <li><strong>Those who labor in the Word may be supported</strong> — freely, from a willing heart, as Paul taught (Galatians 6:6; 2 Corinthians 9:7) — not as a tax, not with a curse, and not for a building.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="cod-body">That is a tithe the widow eats from, not one she pays into.</p>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Ten</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Is the Harm?</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">So what? The church has bills to pay. But Paul said the fight is "against principalities, against powers" (Ephesians 6:12), and Yahushua named one of them Himself. Mammon is the only power He ever set beside the Father as a rival master (Matthew 6:24). "The love of money is the root of all evil," Paul wrote, and some who coveted it "erred from the faith" (1 Timothy 6:10).</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Look at what the church's tithe has done. It taught the believer that he is not the temple, so he looks for Yahuah in a building. It taught him he is not a priest, so he pays another man to be one. And it taught him that his duty is paid in full once the envelope is in the plate, while the widow and the fatherless in his own town are left to someone else. When Babylon falls, her merchants weep, and the last item on the list of her goods is "slaves, and souls of men" (Revelation 18:13).</p>
      <p class="cod-body">And notice what plays while the plates go by. Just as at the altar call in Chapter Six, the offering comes with music — a soft song to set the mood, so the hand reaches for the wallet while the heart is stirred. Chapter Two of this series, <em>Music: The Sound of Self-Worship</em>, showed the pattern on the plain of Dura, where the music was the signal to bow (Daniel 3:5): "The music did not express the worship — it caused it." At the altar call the music moves people to walk forward. At the offering it moves them to give.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Amos saw the same scene in Israel: offerings, songs, and the poor shut out. "Though ye offer me burnt offerings and your meat offerings, I will not accept them … Take thou away from me the noise of thy songs; for I will not hear the melody of thy viols" (Amos 5:22-23). Why? Because "they turn aside the poor in the gate" (Amos 5:12). <strong>Offerings with music, while the poor wait at the gate — Yahuah called it noise.</strong></p>
      <p class="cod-pullquote">Mammon does not need to steal the tithe. He only needs to change the address on the envelope.</p>
      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>
      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">Part Eleven</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Verdict</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>
      <p class="cod-body">The church's tithe is not a doctrine anyone fights over. It is a practice — a church thing — and so few ever check it against the Law it claims to come from. Every church says it honors Yahuah's Word. Then it calls itself the storehouse, calls its pastor the priest, calls its building the house of God, and sends Yahuah's tenth to all three.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Look at the root. A manual that called the prophets "your high priests." A bishop who said the clergy are the Levites. An emperor who gave the church buildings and an income. Councils and kings who made the tithe a tax, and Protestant churches that jailed men for not paying it. A businessman's pamphlets, a storehouse teaching, a tax receipt from Caesar, a building fund, and a seed-faith promise. Every piece moved the tithe further from the temple Yahuah lives in, the priests He made, and the widow at the gate.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">And it is the only piece of the Law most of these churches kept. Yahushua spoke to men who did the same thing: "ye pay tithe of mint and anise and cummin, and have omitted the weightier matters of the law, judgment, mercy, and faith" (Matthew 23:23). When a church throws out the Sabbath, the feasts, and the commandments, and keeps only the tenth, the question is no longer what Yahuah said. It is who benefits.</p>
      <p class="cod-body">Revelation has a word for a church that grew rich this way. To Laodicea, Yahushua said, "Because thou sayest, I am rich, and increased with goods, and have need of nothing; and knowest not that thou art wretched, and miserable, and poor, and blind, and naked: I counsel thee to buy of me gold tried in the fire" (Revelation 3:17-18). And over the city whose merchants traded in "souls of men," the voice from heaven still calls: "Come out of her, my people" (Revelation 18:4).</p>
      <p class="cod-pullquote">You are the temple. You are the priest. The widow is at your gate. That is the storehouse Yahuah meant.</p>

      <div class="cod-end"><span class="cod-end-mark">◆</span></div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

